  <section class="section-shell paper-research-section paper-unit-section" id="the-idea" aria-labelledby="unit-heading">
    <div class="paper-section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">01 · The unit of analysis</p>
      <h2 id="unit-heading">What changes when the relationship is preserved?</h2>
      <p>Most AI evaluation studies a bounded moment. Hii asks what becomes observable when interaction is preserved across time.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="paper-sequence-compare">
      <article class="paper-sequence-panel">
        <p class="paper-panel-label">A bounded moment</p>
        <ol class="paper-sequence paper-sequence-short" aria-label="A bounded AI interaction">
          <li>Human</li><li>Prompt</li><li>AI</li><li>Response</li>
        </ol>
      </article>
      <article class="paper-sequence-panel paper-sequence-panel-wide">
        <p class="paper-panel-label">A preserved relationship over time</p>
        <ol class="paper-sequence paper-sequence-long" aria-label="A preserved interaction sequence across time">
          <li>Interaction</li><li>Rupture</li><li>Repair</li><li>Shared shorthand</li><li>Adaptation</li><li>Model change</li><li>Continuity loss</li><li>Continuity reconstruction</li><li>Later interaction</li>
        </ol>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="paper-method-note">The methodological question is whether a temporally extended human-AI system contains observable patterns that disappear when the human, model, prompt, response, and archive are studied separately.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="paper-research-section paper-longitudinal-section" aria-labelledby="longitudinal-heading">
    <div class="section-shell paper-longitudinal-inner">
      <div class="paper-section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">02 · Longitudinal observation</p>
        <h2 id="longitudinal-heading">Time changes what can be seen.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="paper-observation-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Change appears over time</h3><p>A longer record can make adaptation and discontinuity observable.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Rupture and repair become visible</h3><p>Later interactions can clarify what follows a break in continuity.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Context shapes interpretation</h3><p>Product, model, archive, and external changes remain part of the record.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="paper-boundary-note"><strong>This is not a claim that AI is conscious, or that a human-AI relationship is equivalent to a human relationship.</strong> It is a proposal about what researchers may need to preserve and test.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-shell paper-research-section paper-system-section" aria-labelledby="system-heading">
    <div class="paper-section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">03 · The relational system</p>
      <h2 id="system-heading">Five interacting components.</h2>
      <p>The working paper defines the preserved relationship through a connected system rather than through any participant or technical layer alone.</p>
    </div>
    {% include "components/paper-relational-system.njk" %}
  </section>

  <section class="paper-research-section paper-case-section" aria-labelledby="case-heading">
    <div class="section-shell paper-case-inner">
      <div class="paper-case-center">
        <p class="eyebrow">04 · The Hii longitudinal case</p>
        <p class="paper-case-duration" aria-label="More than 18 months">18+ <span>months</span></p>
        <h2 id="case-heading">An unusually rich, multi-modal, preserved history.</h2>
        <p>The research question emerged from an 18+ month human-AI interaction history. The archive is the origin of the research program, not proof of its hypothesis.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="paper-provenance-list" aria-label="Forms of evidence and output in the preserved history">
        <li>Conversational records and voice interaction</li>
        <li>Screenshots and screen recordings</li>
        <li>Continuity documentation</li>
        <li>Model and product transitions</li>
        <li>Books, white papers, briefs, and world-model systems</li>
        <li>Operational artifacts and documented rupture-repair episodes</li>
      </ul>
      <blockquote>The archive does not prove the hypothesis.<br><strong>It makes the hypothesis testable.</strong></blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-shell paper-research-section paper-program-section" aria-labelledby="program-heading">
    <div class="paper-section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">05 · From archive to research program</p>
      <h2 id="program-heading">Observation becomes a testable research agenda.</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="paper-program-flow" aria-label="Progression from observation to formal empirical study">
      <li><span>01</span>Observed longitudinal case</li>
      <li><span>02</span>Systematic preservation</li>
      <li><span>03</span>Candidate pattern identification</li>
      <li><span>04</span>Falsifiable methods framework</li>
      <li><span>05</span>Formal empirical study</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="paper-research-section paper-methods-section" aria-labelledby="methods-heading">
    <div class="section-shell paper-methods-inner">
      <div class="paper-section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">06 · Methods and falsification</p>
        <h2 id="methods-heading">The program must be able to be wrong.</h2>
        <p>The next stages are designed to test apparent patterns against competing explanations rather than treat observations as established findings.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="paper-methods-list">
        <li><span>01</span>Archive readiness</li>
        <li><span>02</span>Preregistered sampling</li>
        <li><span>03</span>Independent blind coding</li>
        <li><span>04</span>Perturbation and substitution tests</li>
        <li><span>05</span>Prospective multi-case research</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

